 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot C \cdot S)$, where $N$ is the number of fragments, $C$ is the number of cones per fragment (typically 5-9 for diffuse, 1 for specular), and $S$ is the number of steps per cone. Step count depends on max distance and cone aperture [S1][S2, unofficial][S13].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot C \cdot S)$, where $N$ is the number of fragments, $C$ is the number of cones per fragment (typically 5-9 for diffuse, 1 for specular), and $S$ is the number of steps per cone. Step count depends on max distance and cone aperture [G].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$, where $V$ is the volume of the voxel grid. A $512^3$ RGBA16 grid requires ~2.5GB VRAM [S16, unofficial]. Sparse octrees reduce this but add traversal overhead [S3, S17, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Real-time on modern GPUs (25-70 fps on GTX 480 for complex scenes) [S1]. Performance is highly dependent on voxel resolution and the number of traced cones [S1][S19, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles dynamic scenes, supports glossy/specular reflections, provides soft shadows, and is view-independent for the GI component [S1, S13][S17, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Memory intensive at high resolutions, prone to light leaking with isotropic mipmaps, blocky artifacts at low resolutions, and limited light bounces without temporal accumulation [S1][S3, S15, S20, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Real-time on modern GPUs (25-70 fps on GTX 480 for complex scenes) [S1, unofficial]. Performance is highly dependent on voxel resolution and the number of traced cones [S1, unofficial][S19, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles dynamic scenes, supports glossy/specular reflections, provides soft shadows, and is view-independent for the GI component [S1, S13, unofficial][S17, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Memory intensive at high resolutions, prone to light leaking with isotropic mipmaps, blocky artifacts at low resolutions, and limited light bounces without temporal accumulation [S1, unofficial][S3, S15, S20, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Voxel Cone Tracing was introduced by Cyril Crassin, Fabrice Neyret, Miguel Sainz, Simon Green, and Elmar Eisemann from INRIA and NVIDIA in 2011 [S1, S13]. It was presented at SIGGRAPH and later popularized by NVIDIA as "VXGI" (Voxel Global Illumination) [S3, S17, unofficial]. The technique builds on earlier work in voxel rendering and beam tracing, adapting the concept of beam tracing (bundling rays into cones) to a pre-filtered voxel grid for real-time performance [S2, unofficial][S13]. It addresses the limitations of precomputed radiosity (static scenes) and screen-space techniques (no off-screen GI) [S2, unofficial][S13].
